From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CeraLive <dev@ceralive.tv>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 09:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 21/23] media: rockchip: rkvenc: release only what the task actually acquired

MOTIVATION. Arming 0013's fail_clock_enable_once on a real Rock
5B+ produced 'WARNING: bad unlock balance detected!' from
rkvenc_task_finish(), DEBUG_RWSEMS_WARN_ON with the reset group's
rwsem count at 0xffffffffffffff00, and 'Runtime PM usage count
underflow!' -- for the rest of the boot, not just for that task.
rkvenc_hw_run() acquires two runtime-PM references, a wakeup
source, three clocks and the reset group's read lock, and unwinds
every one of them itself on failure; rkvenc_task_finish() released
the same set UNCONDITIONALLY, guarded only by mpp->reset_group
being non-NULL -- a static device-topology fact that says nothing
about what this task did. A refused task still reaches
rkvenc_task_finish() through the worker's own failure path, so
every early exit double-released. The injected fault only makes it
reachable on demand: a genuine clk_prepare_enable() or PM-resume
failure takes exactly the same path.

BEHAVIOUR. One task-state bit, TASK_STATE_HW_HELD, records the
acquisition. rkvenc_hw_run() sets it immediately after the last
acquire and clears it at err_pm, the common tail of all three of
its error labels, so the bit is true exactly when the function
returned holding the set. rkvenc_task_finish() takes it with
test_and_clear_bit() and skips the whole hardware teardown when it
was not set -- which also makes the teardown single-shot, so an IRQ
and a timeout racing to finish one task release once. The bit is
set BEFORE the timeout work is scheduled and before the start
register is written, because either can hand the task to
rkvenc_task_finish() on another CPU immediately. A task hw_run
refused is now also marked abort_request before it is woken, so a
POLL waiter takes rkvenc_wait_result()'s -ENODEV arm instead of
being handed zeroed status registers as a clean encode.

NON-GOALS. Does not change what rkvenc_hw_run() acquires, the order
it acquires in, or what its own unwinds release -- 0015's error
paths are correct in isolation and are untouched. Does not weaken
0014's quiesce/drain or 0020's per-core requiesce. Does not touch
the IOMMU activate/deactivate pair, which hw_run already owns on
both sides. No new lock, no new wait, and no fault-injection knob.

PROVENANCE. First-party CeraLive fix. The release half is 0001's as
imported; 0015 made the failing acquire reachable by propagating
returns that were previously discarded, and did not create the
asymmetry. No upstream counterpart exists.

EVIDENCE POINTER. Root-caused from a REAL Rock 5B+
tests/rkvenc-fault-qa.sh --case fail-clock-enable transcript. That
harness already asserts the correct behaviour with its post-fault
qa_encode, so no test expectation changes: the driver was wrong,
not the test. Build-clean; hardware re-verification is pending.
See docs/BOARD-QUALIFICATION.md.

First-party: authored by CeraLive against v7.1.7, with no upstream
counterpart in rockchip-rk3588-mainline-patches. The source of record is
ceralive/0021-rkvenc-balanced-hw-run-teardown.patch; patches/ is generated from it by
scripts/build-series.py, and scripts/verify-payload-parity.py holds it to the
same added/removed-line parity the upstream lane gets.

NOT upstream-bound: this targets the CeraLive device tree only and is not a
submission to linux-media, linux-rockchip or the fork parent. No Signed-off-by
is added, because a DCO assertion belongs to whoever actually submits it.

---
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_hw.c b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_hw.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_hw.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_hw.c
@@ -546,6 +546,16 @@ int rkvenc_hw_run(struct rkvenc_dev *enc, struct rkvenc_mpp_task *mpp_task)
 	if (enc->reset_group)
 		down_read(&enc->reset_group->rw_sem);
 
+	/*
+	 * Everything rkvenc_task_finish() releases is now held: both runtime-PM
+	 * references, the wakeup source, the three clocks, and -- on a board
+	 * that has one -- the reset group's read lock. Recorded HERE, before
+	 * the timeout work is scheduled and before the start register is
+	 * written, because either of those can hand this task to
+	 * rkvenc_task_finish() on another CPU the instant it happens.
+	 */
+	set_bit(TASK_STATE_HW_HELD, &mpp_task->state);
+
 	/* Match BSP: ensure IOMMU is attached and fault handler is active */
 	ret = rkvenc_iommu_attach(enc->iommu_info);
 	if (ret) {
@@ -626,6 +636,14 @@ err_unlock:
 		up_read(&enc->reset_group->rw_sem);
 	rkvenc_hw_clk_off(enc);
 err_pm:
+	/*
+	 * The common tail of all three unwinds, so one clear covers every one
+	 * of them -- including the two that jump straight here and never took
+	 * the reset-group lock at all. Past this point this function has given
+	 * back everything it acquired, and rkvenc_task_finish() must not give
+	 * any of it back a second time.
+	 */
+	clear_bit(TASK_STATE_HW_HELD, &mpp_task->state);
 	pm_relax(enc->dev);
 	pm_runtime_mark_last_busy(enc->dev);
 	pm_runtime_put_autosuspend(enc->dev);
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_hw.h b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_hw.h
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_hw.h
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_hw.h
@@ -228,6 +228,15 @@ enum {
 	TASK_STATE_FINISH	= 6,
 	TASK_STATE_DONE		= 7,
 	TASK_STATE_ABORT	= 8,
+	/*
+	 * Set by rkvenc_hw_run() only once every resource rkvenc_task_finish()
+	 * releases is actually held, and cleared again on each of its error
+	 * unwinds. It is the ONLY thing that authorises the teardown half of
+	 * that pair: a task hw_run REFUSED still reaches rkvenc_task_finish()
+	 * through the worker, and the release side has no other way to tell a
+	 * task that ran from one that never started.
+	 */
+	TASK_STATE_HW_HELD	= 9,
 };
 
 /* ---- Clock info ---- */
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_service.c b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_service.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_service.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_service.c
@@ -861,6 +861,16 @@ void rkvenc_task_worker_default(struct kthread_work *work)
 				dev_err(target->dev,
 					"hw_run failed: %d, failing task %d\n",
 					run_ret, pending_task->task_index);
+				/*
+				 * The task never reached the hardware, so its
+				 * class registers still hold what userspace
+				 * wrote and its status words are zeros. Marked
+				 * aborted BEFORE it is woken, so a POLL waiter
+				 * takes rkvenc_wait_result()'s -ENODEV arm
+				 * instead of being handed those zeros as a
+				 * clean encode.
+				 */
+				atomic_inc(&pending_task->abort_request);
 				set_bit(TASK_STATE_DONE, &pending_task->state);
 				set_bit(target->core_id, &queue->core_idle);
 				rkvenc_task_finish(pending_task->session,
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_task.c b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_task.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_task.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_task.c
@@ -83,6 +83,27 @@ int rkvenc_task_finish(struct rkvenc_session *session,
 	struct rkvenc_taskqueue *queue = mpp->queue;
 	int ret;
 
+	/*
+	 * EVERY release below is conditional on this task having ACQUIRED, and
+	 * that is the whole point. Five unwind paths reach rkvenc_hw_run()'s
+	 * error labels and two more return outright, and a task it refused
+	 * still arrives here through the worker's own failure path -- so an
+	 * unconditional teardown gave back a reset-group read
+	 * lock that was never taken (`bad unlock balance`, rwsem count
+	 * underflowing to 0xffffffff_ffffff00 for the rest of the boot),
+	 * disabled clocks that were never enabled, and dropped runtime-PM
+	 * references hw_run had already dropped itself ("Runtime PM usage count
+	 * underflow!"). `mpp->reset_group` cannot decide it: that is a static
+	 * device-topology fact, true on every board that wires the resets, and
+	 * says nothing about what THIS task did.
+	 *
+	 * test_and_clear makes it single-shot as well, so a second call on the
+	 * same task -- an IRQ and a timeout racing to finish it -- releases
+	 * once.
+	 */
+	if (!test_and_clear_bit(TASK_STATE_HW_HELD, &task->state))
+		goto done;
+
 	/*
 	 * A failed readback means the status registers userspace is about to be
 	 * handed are STALE, not merely unread -- so the error is recorded on the
@@ -118,6 +139,7 @@ int rkvenc_task_finish(struct rkvenc_session *session,
 	if (mpp->iommu_info && mpp->iommu_info->pdev)
 		pm_runtime_put_sync(&mpp->iommu_info->pdev->dev);
 
+done:
 	set_bit(TASK_STATE_FINISH, &task->state);
 	set_bit(TASK_STATE_DONE, &task->state);
 
